  "ac-mirage-baghdad": {
    "title": "The House of Wisdom: Abbasid Baghdad",
    "subtitle": "When Baghdad was the world's center of knowledge.",
    "mediaType": "game",
    "mediaTitle": "Assassin's Creed Mirage",
    "quickRealityCheck": "9th-century Baghdad, with its House of Wisdom, was the center of science and philosophy, while the Abbasid Caliphate was at the peak of its power. How accurately does Mirage recreate this Golden Age?",
    "mediaConnection": "Assassin’s Creed Mirage reconstructs 9th-century Baghdad based on archaeological data, showcasing its intellectual vibrancy.",
    "mediaChanged": "The main story (Hidden Ones vs Order) is fictional. However, the political tensions, the Zanj Rebellion (slave revolts), and the rising power of Turkish guards (Mamluks) are entirely historical.",
    "whatWeSee": "A massive library, observatory, and scientific center where scholars debate in every corner.",
    "realHistory": "Baghdad, founded by Caliph Al-Mansur as the 'City of Peace,' was the political and cultural capital of the ancient world. At its heart was the 'Bayt al-Hikma' (House of Wisdom). During the Islamic Golden Age, Baghdad housed the world's largest libraries and brightest minds.\n\n### The House of Wisdom\nIt was a research center where Greek, Indian, and Persian texts were translated into Arabic, preserving the ancient world's heritage. Aristo's logic and Euclid's geometry were synthesized with Indian concepts like 'zero.' For each translated book, authors were said to be given its weight in gold.\n\n### The Round City\nThe 'Round City' was an engineering marvel with three concentric walls and the Caliph's palace at the center. Mirage reflects this circular structure and the dense, cosmopolitan atmosphere with great accuracy. The city was fed by Dicle (Tigris) waters through advanced water management systems.\n\n### Political Intrigue\nBy the mid-9th century, Abbasid authority was shaken by power struggles, particularly during the 'Samarra Period.' Turkish soldiers in the guard became kingmakers. The 'Hidden Ones' fiction reflects this real political chaos and street-level unrest.\n\n### The Paper Revolution\nBaghdad's rise was fueled by paper technology learned from the Chinese after the Battle of Talas in 751. Cheap paper allowed for easy copying of books and a massive intellectual explosion. The booksellers' market (Suq al-Warraqin) was the city's vibrant heart.\n\n### Astronomy and Time\nScholars studied the sky for both religious and scientific reasons. Observatories calculated the Earth's circumference and updated star maps. Devices like the astrolabe were perfected for navigation and timekeeping, influencing European science centuries later.\n\n### Global Trade Hub\nBaghdad was the meeting point of silk from China, spices from India, and gold from Africa. The Tigris linked the city to the Indian Ocean. Merchants used early forms of 'checks' (sakk) for transcontinental trade without carrying cash, making it a truly global metropolis.\n\n### Daily Life and Hospitals\nBaghdad was a heterogeneous city where Muslims, Christians, Jews, and Zoroastrians lived together. Hammams, parks, and hospitals (Bimaristan) showed a social welfare level unseen elsewhere. Baghdad hospitals were the first examples of modern medical schools.\n\n### Abbasid Cuisine\nThe city was a gastronomic peak. Spices, meat-cooking techniques, and sherbets spread from Baghdad to the entire Islamic world and Europe. Dining was an art and a matter of social etiquette for Baghdad's elite, setting traditions that continue today.\n\n### The End: 1258 Mongol Invasion\nThe Golden Age ended in 1258 when Hulagu Khan's Mongol army destroyed the city. Libraries were burned, and it was said the Tigris ran black with ink. While the invasion destroyed Baghdad's leadership, the knowledge spread to Cairo and Samarkand.\n\n### A Civilizational Light\nAbbasid Baghdad is one of humanity's brightest links. Mirage allows us to witness one of history's greatest intellectual revolutions. The roots of many modern mathematical and scientific concepts are found behind those circular walls, in the dusty shelves of the House of Wisdom.",
    "accuracyNote": "Baghdad's circular plan and the importance of the House of Wisdom are based on historical accounts like those of Al-Yaqubi and Tabari.",
    "whyInteresting": "The word 'algorithm' and the science of algebra are named after Al-Khwarizmi, who worked in Baghdad during this period.",
    "whyItMatters": "Abbasid Baghdad was the most important cultural bridge that preserved and developed ancient knowledge, laying the foundations for modern science and the European Renaissance.",
    "accuracyType": "partly-real",
    "tags": ["golden-age", "assassins-creed-mirage", "science", "islamic-history"]
  }
